cd8 ratio, abnormal pupillary reflexes, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia\n  Favours second: altered level of alertness, confusion, drowsiness, enlarged pupil in one eye, weakness in part of the body\n\nSkull Base Fracture vs Traumatic Subdural Hematoma:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, abnormal pupillary reflexes, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia\n  Favours second: balance issues, breathing problems, changes in vision, coma, disorientation\n\nSkull Base Fracture vs Traumatic Intracerebral Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, abnormal pupillary reflexes, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia\n  Favours second: abnormal cns myelination, ataxia, athetosis, cerebellar atrophy, cerebral atrophy\n\nSkull Base Fracture vs Ruptured Cerebral Aneurysm with Subarachnoid Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, abnormal pupillary reflexes, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia\n  Favours second: anxiety, blind spots, cognitive dysfunction, confusion, decreased consciousness\n\nSkull Base Fracture vs Hemorrhagic Transformation of Prior Cerebral Infarction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, abnormal pupillary reflexes, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia\n  Favours second: altered consciousness, aphasia, hemiparesis, nausea and vomiting, neurological deterioration\n\nTraumatic Epidural Hematoma vs Traumatic Subdural Hematoma:\n  Favours first:  altered level of alertness, confusion, drowsiness, enlarged pupil in one eye, nausea or vomiting\n  Favours second: balance issues, breathing problems, changes in vision, coma, disorientation\n\nTraumatic Epidural Hematoma vs Traumatic Intracerebral Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  altered level of alertness, drowsiness, enlarged pupil in one eye, headache, nausea or vomiting\n  Favours second: abnormal cns myelination, ataxia, athetosis, autosomal recessive inheritance, cerebellar