l Intestinal Obstruction (Malrotation/Volvulus) vs Duodenal Atresia in Down Syndrome:\n  Favours first:  abdominal distension, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal rectum morphology, abnormality of metabolism/homeostasis\n  Favours second: abnormality of cardiovascular system morphology, abnormality of immune system physiology, abnormality of the dentition, abnormality of the fontanelles or cranial sutures, abnormality of the lymphatic system\n\nNeonatal Mechanical Intestinal Obstruction (Malrotation/Volvulus) vs Meconium Ileus in Down Syndrome without Cystic Fibrosis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal distension, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal rectum morphology, abnormality of the hepatic vasculature\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nDuodenal Atresia in Down Syndrome vs Meconium Ileus in Down Syndrome without Cystic Fibrosis:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of cardiovascular system morphology, abnormality of immune system physiology, abnormality of the fontanelles or cranial sutures, abnormality of the lymphatic system, acute megakaryocytic leukemia\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nRelated to Neonatal Mechanical Intestinal Obstruction (Malrotation/Volvulus): chronic intestinal pseudoobstruction, duodenal obstruction, functional colonic disease\n\nRelated to Duodenal Atresia in Down Syndrome: total autosomal trisomy\n\nRelated to Meconium Ileus in Down Syndrome without Cystic Fibrosis: meconium ileus, perinatal disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  He was born at 37 weeks of gestation to a 38-year-old G3P3 woman → increased body weight → Meconium Ileus in Down Syndrome without Cystic Fibrosis\n  The pregnancy was uncomplicated and the patient