s from hiking (B3.2), while reducing the likelihood of arthropod bite panniculitis (B3.3) and vector-related diseases (Lyme, Leishmaniasis). Autoimmune blistering disease remains low due to inconsistent lesion features."]}, "B4.3": {"id": "B4.3", "label": "Insect Bite Hypersensitivity Panniculitis (e.g., sandfly-induced)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.004293470644885254, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "B4", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The analysis of the patient's recent azithromycin use, thick plaque morphology, and clinical context suggests moderate support for drug-induced Type IV hypersensitivity (B3.1) and allergic contact dermatitis from hiking (B3.2), while reducing the likelihood of arthropod bite panniculitis (B3.3) and vector-related diseases (Lyme, Leishmaniasis). Autoimmune blistering disease remains low due to inconsistent lesion features."]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 8, "bundle_size": 12, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of hundreds of prior insect bites required for hypersensitivity panniculitis at B3.3 undermine its plausibility?", "result_summary": "The analysis of the patient's recent azithromycin use, thick plaque morphology, and clinical context suggests moderate support for drug-induced Type IV hypersensitivity (B3.1) and allergic contact dermatitis from hiking (B3.2), while reducing the likelihood of arthropod bite panniculitis (B3.3) and vector-related diseases (Lyme, Leishmaniasis). Autoimmune blistering disease remains low due to inconsistent lesion features."}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 9, "bundle_size": 12, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of annular/ring pattern in lesions argue against B4.1 despite outdoor exposure?", "result_summary": "Th