{"case_id": "MB_20", "timestep": 2, "case_summary": "A 57-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with weakness for the past 6 months with symptoms that have worsened recently. She feels fatigued, depressed, and has gained 10 pounds which she attributes to feeling too weak to go to the gym. She was recently treated for an ear infection with an antibiotic which she completed 3 weeks ago, and experienced a rash which she believes was an allergic reaction to her treatment. The patient has a medical history of diabetes mellitus that is well-controlled with insulin and metformin. She has been admitted multiple times for hypoglycemia secondary to missing meals. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 127/68 mmHg, pulse is 80/min, respirations are 17/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for 2/5 strength in the patient's upper and lower extremities, sparse fine hair on her body, and a rash on her face and arms. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L K+: 3.3 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L HCO3-: 25 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 70 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.2 mg/dL AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L A muscle biopsy is obtained as seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this patient's presentation?\n\nOptions:\nA. Dermatomyositis\nB. Hypokalemia\nC. Hypothyroidism\nD. Inclusion body myositis\nE. Polymyositis\n", "root": {"label": "Chronic Progressive Generalized Myopathy with Concurrent Systemic Features and Uncharacterised Muscle Biopsy Abnormality", "time_course": "chronic", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.65, "supporting_facts": ["6-month progressive generalized weakness", "2/5 strength bilaterally in upper and lower extremities", "Weight gain attributed to reduced activity in myopathy context", "Concomitant rash (possibly paraneoplastic or dermatomyositis-related)", "Hypokalemia (K+: 3.3 mEq/L) in otherwise euthyroid/euhydrated state", "Muscle biopsy performed (Figure A) but not described in text"], "excluded_candidates": ["Drug-induced myopathy (antibiotic-related): likely, but resolved after 3-week drug course and ongoing myopathy suggests more persistent mechanism", "Diabetic autonomic neuropathy: could present with weakness but wouldn't explain 2/5 myopathy pattern or rash", "Hypothyroid myopathy: plausible given systemic features but lacks direct evidence (no TSH/Free T4 reported yet)", "Metabolic myopathy including carnitine deficiency: supported by myopathy but needs more evidence"], "alarm_features": ["Chronic progressive proximal muscle weakness", "Systemic features (weight gain, depression, fatigue, rash)", "Persistent hypokalemia despite eunatremia and stable diabetes control"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Inflammatory/Immune-Mediated Myopathy", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.368421052631579, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The facial/arms rash is more characteristic of inflammatory myopathies (dermatomyositis), providing moderate support for B1 over B2 and B3.", "Lack of hypothyroid-specific features reduces strength of support for B3, but doesn't eliminate it."], "evidence_against": ["The lack of diuretic use or recent drug exposure supports hypokalemia as a primary metabolic issue (B2), but not strongly conclusive.", "Absence of elevated muscle enzymes reduces suspicion for inflammatory myopathies but doesn't rule them out definitively."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Rash distribution (e.g., heliotrope, Gottron's papules or non-specific)", "History of photosensitivity or Raynaud's phenomenon", "Autoimmune disease family history"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Elevated creatine kinase (CK)", "Antinuclear antibody (ANA) panel", "Muscle biopsy (Figure A findings)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Metabolic/Channelopathy Myopathy with Hypokalemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.26315789473684215, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The lack of diuretic use or recent drug exposure supports hypokalemia as a primary metabolic issue (B2), but not strongly conclusive.", "Lack of hypothyroid-specific features reduces strength of support for B3, but doesn't eliminate it."], "evidence_against": ["Normal labs don't align with typical metabolic myopathy presentations like hypokalemic periodic paralysis, modestly weakening B2."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Pattern of hypokalemia (sporadic, episodic, or persistent)", "Family history of similar symptoms or metabolic disorders", "History of diuretic use or high sodium intake"], "requestable_discriminators": ["24-hour urinary potassium", "Renal function tests", "Serum magnesium"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "test_pathway"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Endocrine-Dysfunction Related Myopathy", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.35, "posterior": 0.3684210526315789, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["These features overlap with hypothyroidism, providing moderate support for B3, but require lab confirmation."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of hypothyroid-specific features reduces strength of support for B3, but doesn't eliminate it."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Clinical features of hypothyroidism (cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin)", "Weight gain and depression not explained by other systemic myopathies", "Hair loss pattern (fine/peach-fuzz vs. alopecia areata)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["TSH and Free T4 levels", "Thyroid peroxidase antibodies"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Dermatomyositis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.16374269005847952, "posterior": 0.16374269005847952, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Rash distribution (e.g., heliotrope rash, Gottron's papules, or non-specific)", "History of photosensitivity or Raynaud's phenomenon", "Autoimmune disease family history"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Elevated creatine kinase (CK)", "Antinuclear antibody (ANA) panel showing specific patterns", "Muscle biopsy (Figure A findings) showing myositis with perifascicular atrophy"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Polymyositis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1023391812865497, "posterior": 0.1023391812865497, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Absence of characteristic skin findings (heliotrope/Gottron)", "Presence of symmetric, proximal muscle weakness", "History of systemic autoimmune symptoms like joint pain or fever"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Elevated aldolase or lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)", "Anti-Jo-1/anti-Pm-Scl antibodies", "Muscle biopsy (Figure A) showing inflammatory cell infiltration without perifascicular atrophy"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.35, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Inclusion Body Myositis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1023391812865497, "posterior": 0.1023391812865497, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Slow progression (≥24 months) with asymmetric weakness", "History of difficulty with grip/forearm muscles or quad cramps", "Presence of distal/proximal mixed weakness pattern"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Muscle biopsy (Figure A) showing rimmed vacuoles and CD8+ T-cell infiltration", "Elevated CK without concurrent muscle enzyme elevation (AST/ALT normal)", "Electromyography (EMG) for myopathic features"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "pathology"}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08771929824561404, "posterior": 0.08771929824561404, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of episodic paralysis (paroxysmal, transient weakness)", "Family history of similar paralytic episodes", "Low serum glucose (70 mg/dL) potentially linked to hypokalemic paralytic episodes"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Thyroid function testing (TSH, Free T4 to rule out underlying hyperthyroidism)", "24-hour urinary potassium", "Serum magnesium and creatinine levels"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08771929824561404, "posterior": 0.08771929824561404, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Presence of palmar hyperhidrosis or palpitations", "Known hyperthyroidism or Graves' disease history", "Recent dietary changes (high-carb intake triggering attacks)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Thyroid stimulating hormone receptor antibody testing", "Free T3 and T4 measurements", "EKG for junctional tachycardia or atrial fibrillation"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Gitelman Syndrome (Familial Hypokalemic Tubulopathy)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08771929824561404, "posterior": 0.08771929824561404, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Fatigue worsening with exertion (vs. paroxysmal weakness in channelopathies)", "Family history of salt-wasting syndromes", "History of tetany/parasthesias due to hypomagnesemia"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Serum magnesium (<1.7 mg/dL typically)", "Renal leak indices (urine sodium-to-potassium ratio)", "Genetic testing for SLC12A3 mutations"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "pathology"}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a facial and arm rash in a patient with chronic progressive weakness support an inflammatory/immune-mediated mechanism (as in dermatomyositis/polymyositis) over metabolic or endocrine etiologies?", "result_summary": "The facial/arms rash is more characteristic of inflammatory myopathies (dermatomyositis), providing moderate support for B1 over B2 and B3.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_for", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the persistent hypokalemia (K+ 3.3 mEq/L) in a patient with no current diuretic use or high sodium intake suggest a metabolic/channelopathy mechanism rather than secondary causes like drug allergy?", "result_summary": "The lack of diuretic use or recent drug exposure supports hypokalemia as a primary metabolic issue (B2), but not strongly conclusive.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_against", "B2": "moderate_for", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of weight gain, depression, sparse fine hair, and chronic fatigue align with classic hypothyroid features sufficient to shift priority toward endocrine dysfunction?", "result_summary": "These features overlap with hypothyroidism, providing moderate support for B3, but require lab confirmation.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "CHALLENGE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the normal AST/ALT (12/10 U/L) and lack of reported muscle enzymes/CK in static evidence weaken the likelihood of immune-mediated inflammatory myopathies?", "result_summary": "Absence of elevated muscle enzymes reduces suspicion for inflammatory myopathies but doesn't rule them out definitively.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "CHALLENGE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the normal AST/ALT (12/10 U/L) and euhydration (Na+ 139 mEq/L) inconsistent with a chronic metabolic myopathy like hypokalemic periodic paralysis?", "result_summary": "Normal labs don't align with typical metabolic myopathy presentations like hypokalemic periodic paralysis, modestly weakening B2.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_against", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "CHALLENGE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset of weakness with no reported cold intolerance, delayed skin manifestations, or other hypothyroid-specific features make endocrine dysfunction less likely than alternative explanations?", "result_summary": "Lack of hypothyroid-specific features reduces strength of support for B3, but doesn't eliminate it.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B2": "weak_for", "B3": "moderate_against"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.368421052631579, "B2": 0.26315789473684215, "B3": 0.3684210526315789, "B1.1": 0.16374269005847952, "B1.2": 0.1023391812865497, "B1.3": 0.1023391812865497, "B2.1": 0.08771929824561404, "B2.2": 0.08771929824561404, "B2.3": 0.08771929824561404}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers (airway, respiratory, haemodynamic, neurological, or haemorrhagic) are present, and the clinical features do not immediately suggest time-critical syndromes requiring pre-diagnostic action.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Multiple branches (B1, B2, B3) remain with posterior <0.75 and unresolved dangerous alternatives (B2's subbranches have 0.087 posterior). Key discriminators like TSH/Free T4, creatine kinase, and muscle biopsy analysis have not yet been requested."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.6, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "CHALLENGE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3684210526315789, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 57-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with weakness for the past 6 months with symptoms that have worsened recently. She feels fatigued, depressed, and has gained 10 pounds which she attributes to feeling too weak to go to the gym. She was recently treated for an ear infection with an antibiotic which she completed 3 weeks ago, and experienced a rash which she believes was an allergic reaction to her treatment. The patient has a medical history of diabetes mellitus that is well-controlled with insulin and metformin. She has been admitted multiple times for hypoglycemia secondary to missing meals. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 127/68 mmHg, pulse is 80/min, respirations are 17/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for 2/5 strength in the patient's upper and lower extremities, sparse fine hair on her body, and a rash on her face and arms. Laboratory values are ordered as seen below. Serum: Na+: 139 mEq/L K+: 3.3 mEq/L Cl-: 100 mEq/L HCO3-: 25 mEq/L BUN: 20 mg/dL Glucose: 70 mg/dL Creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL Ca2+: 10.2 mg/dL AST: 12 U/L ALT: 10 U/L A muscle biopsy is obtained as seen in Figure A.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation for this patient's presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Dermatomyositis"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Hypokalemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Hypothyroidism"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Inclusion body myositis"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Polymyositis"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Age and gender: 57-year-old woman", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: progressive generalized weakness for 6 months, worsening recently", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Patient feels fatigued and depressed", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Weight gain of 10 pounds attributed to decreased exercise", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Recent treatment for ear infection with antibiotic completed 3 weeks ago", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Rash associated with antibiotic treatment (self-attributed allergic reaction)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: well-controlled diabetes mellitus with insulin/metformin", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: recurrent hypoglycemia admissions due to missed meals", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Temperature: 99.5°F (37.5°C)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "BP: 127/68 mmHg", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pulse: 80/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E12", "kind": "direct", "content": "Respirations: 17/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E13", "kind": "direct", "content": "Oxygen saturation: 98% on room air", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E14", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam finding: 2/5 strength in upper and lower extremities", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E15", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam finding: sparse fine body hair", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E16", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam finding: facial and arm rash", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E17", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum sodium: 139 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E18", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum potassium: 3.3 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E19", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum chloride: 100 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E20", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum bicarbonate: 25 mEq/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E21", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum BUN: 20 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E22", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum glucose: 70 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E23", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum creatinine: 1.1 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E24", "kind": "direct", "content": "Serum calcium: 10.2 mg/dL", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E25", "kind": "direct", "content": "AST: 12 U/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E26", "kind": "direct", "content": "ALT: 10 U/L", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E27", "kind": "image_reference", "content": "Muscle biopsy is obtained as seen in Figure A — image not available in text form", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Pituitary hypothyroidism', 'Hypothyroidism'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=86%, source=both]\n\nDermatomyositis vs Polymyositis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormality of the nail, acrocyanosis\n  Favours second: abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology, anorexia\n\nDermatomyositis vs Inclusion Body Myositis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, distal muscle weakness, hyporeflexia, quadriceps muscle weakness, ragged-red muscle fibers\n\nDermatomyositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles\n\nDermatomyositis vs Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, decreased urinary potassium, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue\n\nDermatomyositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Familial Hypokalemic Tubulopathy):\n  Favours first:  abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormality of the nail\n  Favours second: abnormal t-wave, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance, blurred vision, cerebral calcification\n\nPolymyositis vs Inclusion Body Myositis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, distal muscle weakness, feeding difficulties in infancy, hyporeflexia, inflammatory myopathy\n\nPolymyositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles\n\nPolymyositis vs Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: decreased urinary potassium, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, graves disease\n\nPolymyositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Familial Hypokalemic Tubulopathy):\n  Favours first:  abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal t-wave, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance, blurred vision, cerebral calcification\n\nInclusion Body Myositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2):\n  Favours first:  autoimmunity, distal muscle weakness, dysphagia, elevated serum creatine kinase, feeding difficulties in infancy\n  Favours second: episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles, incomplete penetrance\n\nInclusion Body Myositis vs Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  autoimmunity, autosomal dominant inheritance, distal muscle weakness, dysphagia, elevated serum creatine kinase\n  Favours second: decreased urinary potassium, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, graves disease\n\nInclusion Body Myositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Familial Hypokalemic Tubulopathy):\n  Favours first:  abnormal muscle fiber morphology, autoimmunity, autosomal dominant inheritance, distal muscle weakness, dysphagia\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal t-wave, arthralgia, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance\n\nHypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2) vs Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles, incomplete penetrance, muscle weakness, myopathy\n  Favours second: decreased urinary potassium, graves disease, hyperhidrosis, hyperkalemia, hyperthyroidism"}
