ow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=89%, source=both]\n\nDermatomyositis vs Polymyositis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormality of the nail, acrocyanosis\n  Favours second: abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology, anorexia\n\nDermatomyositis vs Inclusion Body Myositis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, distal muscle weakness, hyporeflexia, quadriceps muscle weakness, ragged-red muscle fibers\n\nDermatomyositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles\n\nDermatomyositis vs Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, decreased urinary potassium, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue\n\nDermatomyositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Familial Hypokalemic Tubulopathy):\n  Favours first:  abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormality of the nail\n  Favours second: abnormal t-wave, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance, blurred vision, cerebral calcification\n\nDermatomyositis vs Hypothyroidism-related Myopathy:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: areflexia, autosomal recessive inheritance, camptodactyly of finger, cleft palate, congenital onset\n\nDermatomyositis vs Hyperthyroidism-related Myopathy:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: areflexia, autosomal recessive inheritance, camptodactyly of finger, cleft palate, congenital onset\n\nPolymyositis vs Inclusion Body Myositis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, distal muscle weakness, feeding difficulties in infancy, hyporeflexia, inflammatory myopathy\n\nPolymyositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis (Type 1 or 2):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles\n\nPolymyositis vs Thyrotoxic Periodic Paralysis (Hyperthyroidism-related hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: decreased urinary potassium, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, graves disease\n\nPolymyositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Familial Hypokalemic Tubulopathy):\n  Favours first:  abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal t-wave, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance, blurred vision, cerebral calcification\n\nPolymyositis vs Hypothyroidism-related Myopathy:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: areflexia, autosomal recessive inheritance, camptodactyly of finger, cleft palate, congenital onset\n\nPolymyositis vs Hyperthyroidism-related Myopathy:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: areflexia, autosomal recessive inheritance, camptodactyly of finger, cleft palate, congenital onset"}
