{"state": {"case_id": "MB_19", "timestep": 5, "case_summary": "A 27-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician for an abnormal vaginal discharge. The patient has engaged in unprotected intercourse with 12 different partners. She has a medical history of asthma, IV drug abuse, and depression. She has 1 to 2 alcoholic drinks per day. She feels ashamed of her behavior and is requesting treatment for her condition and advice for safe sex. The patient is given antibiotics and is advised on safe sex practices. The patient presents to the emergency department 3 days later with a complaint of a rash. She states that every time she has sex a rash has emerged on her skin. Her temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 127/68 mmHg, pulse is 91/min, respirations are 14/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. A physical exam is notable for the finding in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?\n\nOptions:\nA. Antigen exposure\nB. Drug reaction\nC. Infection\nD. IV drug use\nE. Photosensitivity\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Recurrent Skin Rash with Sexual Activity Trigger, of Uncertain Aetiology, Possibly Allergic or Infectious in Origin, with Uncharacterised Figure A Findings and History of IV Drug Abuse and Multiple Unprotected Partners", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.5, "supporting_facts": ["Follow-up complaint after 3 days: rash", "Rash temporal association: emerges on skin with every sexual encounter", "Exposure context: 12 partners with unprotected intercourse", "Initial treatment: antibiotics prescribed"], "excluded_candidates": ["Acute Stevens-Johnson Syndrome: antibiotic side-effects could explain rash but temporal association with sexual activity conflicts with typical drug eruption", "Secondary Syphilis with Late Jarisch-Herxheimer Reaction: possible infectious mechanism but would require Figure A showing serpiginous lesions or palmo-plantar findings"], "ala