s vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis with Myotonia (Dominant Thomsen-type):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles\n\nDermatomyositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Type II Tubular Hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abnormal eosinophil morphology, abnormal eyelid morphology, abnormal hair quantity, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormality of the nail\n  Favours second: abnormal t-wave, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance, blurred vision, cerebral calcification\n\nPolymyositis vs Inclusion Body Myositis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, distal muscle weakness, feeding difficulties in infancy, hyporeflexia, inflammatory myopathy\n\nPolymyositis vs Andersen-Tawil Syndrome (Mixed Hypokalemic-Hyperkalemic Paroxysmal Myopathy):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology\n  Favours second: antegonial notching of mandible, autosomal dominant inheritance, bidirectional ventricular ectopy, broad forehead, bulbous nose\n\nPolymyositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis with Myotonia (Dominant Thomsen-type):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles\n\nPolymyositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Type II Tubular Hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abnormal atrioventricular conduction, abnormal mitral valve morphology, abnormal muscle fiber morphology, abnormal pulmonary interstitial morphology, abnormal renal tubule morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal t-wave, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance, blurred vision, cerebral calcification\n\nInclusion Body Myositis vs Andersen-Tawil Syndrome (Mixed Hypokalemic-Hyperkalemic Paroxysmal Myopathy):\n  Favours first:  abnormal muscle fiber morphology, autoimmunity, distal muscle weakness, dysphagia, elevated serum creatine kinase\n  Favours second: antegonial notching of mandible, bidirectional ventricular ectopy, broad forehead, bulbous nose, cleft palate\n\nInclusion Body Myositis vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis with Myotonia (Dominant Thomsen-type):\n  Favours first:  autoimmunity, distal muscle weakness, dysphagia, elevated serum creatine kinase, feeding difficulties in infancy\n  Favours second: episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue, fatigable weakness of respiratory muscles, incomplete penetrance\n\nInclusion Body Myositis vs Gitelman Syndrome (Type II Tubular Hypokalemia):\n  Favours first:  abnormal muscle fiber morphology, autoimmunity, autosomal dominant inheritance, distal muscle weakness, dysphagia\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal t-wave, arthralgia, ataxia, autosomal recessive inheritance\n\nAndersen-Tawil Syndrome (Mixed Hypokalemic-Hyperkalemic Paroxysmal Myopathy) vs Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis with Myotonia (Dominant Thomsen-type):\n  Favours first:  antegonial notching of mandible, bidirectional ventricular ectopy, broad forehead, bulbous nose, cleft palate\n  Favours second: abnormal muscle fiber morphology, emg abnormality, episodic flaccid weakness, episodic hypokalemia, exercise-induced muscle fatigue"}
