{"case_id": "MB_21", "timestep": 5, "case_summary": "A 24-year-old man presents to the emergency department with abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. The symptoms began towards the end of running a marathon and persisted after finishing the race. The patient is otherwise healthy and has no other medical problems. His temperature is 98.0°F (36.7°C), blood pressure is 105/71 mmHg, pulse is 113/min, respirations are 19/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals a distended and tender abdomen. A radiograph is performed as seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Cecal volvulus\nB. Ileus\nC. Ogilvie syndrome\nD. Sigmoid volvulus\nE. Small bowel obstruction\n", "root": {"label": "Acute Abdominal Pain Syndrome with Nausea and Vomiting (Post-Exertional Onset) and Uncharacterised Radiograph Abnormality", "time_course": "acute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.5, "supporting_facts": ["Symptoms began towards the end of running a marathon", "Abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting", "Physical examination reveals distended and tender abdomen", "Tachycardia (pulse 113/min with borderline hypotension)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Heat exhaustion with gastrointestinal involvement: normal temperature reduces likelihood", "Exercise-associated hyponatremia: hydration status remains unspecified from history", "Acute mesenteric ischemia: tachycardia raises suspicion but lacks specific imaging findings", "Post-exertional rhabdomyolysis-induced ileus: missing lab values reduce mechanistic link validity"], "alarm_features": ["Distended/tender abdomen", "Tachycardia with relative hypotension", "Post-exertional symptom onset in previously healthy young adult"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Mechanical Bowel Obstruction", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.08670921652272237, "posterior": 0.05987876852403441, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "exp